  "snippet": "#​811 — November 17, 2026 Read on the Web JavaScript Weekly npkill 1.0: Not Just for node_modules Anymore — npx npkill has long offered a way to find and remove waste from node_modules folders. Its first release in two y...",
  "coverImage": "https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/rmedfrw5ofajinij51fv.jpg",
  "readTime": 1,
  "content": "<table align=\"center\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<div>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr>\n<td align=\"left\" style=\"padding-left: 4px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"><p>#​811 — November 17, 2026</p></td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"padding-right: 4px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"><p><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193000/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">Read on the Web</a></p></td>\n</tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0 12px;\"><p>JavaScript Weekly</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192932/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/rmedfrw5ofajinij51fv.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 100%;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192932/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.1em; line-height: 1.4em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"zaldih.com\">npkill 1.0: Not Just for <code>node_modules</code> Anymore</a></span> — <code>npx npkill</code> has long offered a way to find and remove waste from <code>node_modules</code> folders. Its first release in two years now also hunts down caches and build outputs like <code>.next</code> and <code>.turbo</code>, with 'profiles' for 20 ecosystems. If you're new to the tool, <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192933/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">its homepage</a> provides a good introduction.</p>\n<p>Juan Torres </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192931/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\"><img height=\"130\" loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/c_limit,w_480,h_480,q_auto/copm/e001ca9e.png\" style=\"padding-top: 12px; padding-left: 12px; line-height: 100%;\" width=\"130\"/></a>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192931/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"www.meticulous.ai\">Agents Didn’t Clear Your Plate. They Dumped Reviews on It</a></span> — Your agents write PRs in minutes, then eat your afternoon in review. When did ‘saving time’ start to mean babysitting agents’ work? Meticulous shows the exhaustive impact of every PR, with no developer effort. Devs at Dropbox &amp; ElevenLabs have their afternoons back. You can too.</p>\n<p>Meticulous <span style=\"margin-left: 4px; font-size: 0.9em; color: #993 !important; padding: 1px 4px;\">sponsor</span></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192934/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"ui-sdk.dev\">UI SDK: Headless UI Behaviors for React, Vue, Svelte, &amp; Solid</a></span> — A new library from shadcn and Vercel that provides 50+ accessible, low-level behaviors, like roving focus, typeahead, popups, and drag and drop, that you compose into components (for several frameworks, plus Web Components).</p>\n<p>Vercel </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>💡 <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192935/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">𝕏 As shadcn says</a>: <em>\"You and I don't need the same interfaces. We need the same interface behaviors.\"</em> The idea is an agent, say, can generate whatever UI suits a task, with reusable focus, keyboard, and accessibility behaviors built in.</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><strong>IN BRIEF:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193001/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Solid 2.0</a> is now stable, with <code>2.0.0</code> <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193002/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">now on npm</a> and <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193003/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">the docs</a> updated to match. There's no official announcement post (beyond the earlier <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193004/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Solid 2.0 RC posts</a>) so expect more on this soon.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The Chrome team has <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192936/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">proposed a Cross-Origin Storage API</a> for sites to share large assets like AI models and WASM modules, so users would only have to download them once.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The cloud-based JavaScript hosting service <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192938/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\"><em>Val Town</em> is winding down</a> with services ending January 10 and the code being open sourced in February.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The Bun team has published <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192937/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">a dense technical tour of how its in-development ahead-of-time compiler works</a>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><strong>RELEASES:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192939/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Prettier 3.10</a> – The code formatter now supports the latest JavaScript proposals and new Flow syntax, and adds an opt-in plugin that swaps in the Zig-based <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192940/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Yuku</a>'s JS/TS parsers for speed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🎨 <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192941/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">p5.js 2.4</a> – The creative coding library expands its SVG and 3D model support. Here's <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192942/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">a starter sketch</a> if you want to play quickly.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🔒 <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192943/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Node.js v26.13.1 (LTS)</a>, <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192944/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">v24.22.3</a> and <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192945/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">v22.23.5</a> – The <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192946/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">security releases</a> we mentioned last week fix an HTTP/2 flaw that lets clients crash a server and an HTTPS bug that can leak other connections' memory.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192947/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">PixiJS 3D</a> – The popular 2D rendering library's official 3D engine is now in open beta. It's WebGPU-first, with a WebGL 2 fallback.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0;\"><p>📖  Articles and Videos</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192948/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/amlrfsxirjmhqkgohrz0.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 100%;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><span>📊</span> <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192948/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"e18e.dev\">e18e's Ongoing Benchmarks of JavaScript Meta-Frameworks</a></span> — The <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192949/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">e18e</a> performance initiative tracks releases of frameworks across the ecosystem (e.g. Nuxt, Astro, SvelteKit, SolidStart, and more), measuring everything from install size and build times to rendering speed. <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192950/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">This dashboard</a> is the place to dive in.</p>\n<p>e18e </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><span>🤖</span> <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192951/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"developer.chrome.com\">The Top 10 Things AI Gets Wrong in Web Development</a></span> — The Chrome team examines where coding agents fall back on dated techniques, like hand-rolled focus traps instead of <code>inert</code> and JS overlays instead of <code>popover</code>, alongside gaps in passkey implementations and XSS defenses. A handy checklist for humans too.</p>\n<p>Philip Walton (Google) </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192952/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"fandf.co\">AI Security for Modern JavaScript &amp; TypeScript</a></span> — Traditional tools miss logic gaps. SonarQube Hunter Agent catches broken access control in your JS/TS CI/CD pipeline.</p>\n<p>SonarSource <span style=\"margin-left: 4px; font-size: 0.9em; color: #993 !important; padding: 1px 4px;\">sponsor</span></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192953/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"blog.report-uri.com\">How a Third-Party Script Turned Malicious on Major Sites</a></span> — An attacker exploited a flaw in Optimizely's A/B testing platform to inject a (<a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192954/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">now removed</a>) malicious script into pages served to end users. Scott's analysis found it on sites including Zoom, the BBC, and GitLab, and shows how CSP can, and did, help.</p>\n<p>Scott Helme (Report URI) </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>💡 Third-party scripts are an increasingly common attack vector, as <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192955/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Polymarket's users recently found</a>. If you load any, brush up on <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192956/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">CSP</a> and <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192957/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Subresource Integrity</a>, and if you <em>provide</em> them, see <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192958/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">how Fathom secures theirs</a>.</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192959/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"alistair.sh\">How Promises Really Resolve, Explained via a WebKit Bug</a></span> — A contrived promise that should have thrown instead caused Bun and Safari (both of which use <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192960/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">JavaScriptCore</a>) to hang. Alistair fixed the bug and uses it to explain what <code>resolve()</code> actually does and why <code>resolved</code> isn't <em>'a real promise state'</em>.</p>\n<p>Alistair Smith </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192961/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"ftrain.com\">The Web Is More Than Enough</a></span> — A reminder of how the vast array of powerful web platform APIs enables the development of complex apps in the browser, particularly as AI reduces the headaches involved in tying them together.</p>\n<p>Paul Ford </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192962/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"blog.gaborkoos.com\">Your JS Error Is Lying to You</a></span> — Why can <code>JSON.stringify(err)</code> unexpectedly leave you with <code>{}</code>? Gabor presents a tour of what an <code>Error</code> holds, and how much of that makes it through stack traces, <code>cause</code> chains, worker boundaries, and loggers like Pino and Winston.</p>\n<p>Gabor Koos </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192963/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">How 13,000 Local Variables in a Function Overflowed V8's Stack</a> – And why would that ever happen? It was the output of an OCaml to JS transpiler… :-) <cite>Jérôme Vouillon</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192964/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">A Universal XSS in uBlock Origin via Its Element Picker</a> – Leans on an interesting (and now fixed) <code>postMessage</code> and iframe exploit chain. <cite>Smitty van Bodegom</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192965/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Migrating 10,144 Tests from Jest to Vitest in Three Weeks</a> <cite>Daniele Rossino</cite></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0;\"><p>🛠 Code &amp; Tools</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192966/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/pskrn2fbmsv0dydrn175.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 100%;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192966/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"browserpod.io\">BrowserPod 4.0: A Linux-Like MicroVM for the Browser</a></span> — A WASM-powered sandbox that runs Node, npm, git, and bash in the browser. Previously a metered service, v4.0 is free for any use, though it's not open source, and you must load it via their CDN (but no account or API key is needed).</p>\n<p>Leaning Technologies </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>💡 Going somewhat deeper, Nitro creator Pooya Parsa has compiled <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192967/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">the actual Linux kernel to WebAssembly</a> (<a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192968/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">repo</a>). It boots to a shell in under a second in modern browsers, or via <code>npx unkernel</code> <em>(tip: use <code>halt</code> to exit!)</em>.</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192969/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"sentry.io\">Debug Errors and Agents Faster: Sentry Changelog Live</a></span> — A walkthrough of what’s new in debugging, from fixing prod bugs with Seer to tracing your agents. <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192969/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">Register today</a>.</p>\n<p>Sentry <span style=\"margin-left: 4px; font-size: 0.9em; color: #993 !important; padding: 1px 4px;\">sponsor</span></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192970/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"blog.cloudflare.com\">Kitesurf: Cloudflare's Browser Engine Now Open Source</a></span> — An experimental browser for agents we featured in August. Mostly written in TypeScript and runs JavaScript in V8 isolates on <em>Workers</em>, with Rust compiled to WASM for layout and painting. There's no Chromium, and Puppeteer can drive it.</p>\n<p>Cloudflare </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192971/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"github.com\">File Viewer: A Multi-Format File Viewer for Internal Apps</a></span> — Read-only views for Office, PDF/OFD, CAD, archives, email and other complex formats, with integrations for React, Vue, and Svelte. The <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192972/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">docs site</a> has demos showing off the formats covered.</p>\n<p>Flyfish Dev </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192973/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"flashwright.dev\">Flashwright: A Faster, API-Compatible Playwright Fork</a></span> — Keeps Playwright's API and test runner, but reuses a browser context per worker and doesn't wait for animations to settle, plus similar tricks. Benchmarks show a ~2-4x speedup, while <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192974/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">this post explains how it works</a>.</p>\n<p>Codebeast </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192975/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"xyflow.com\">React Flow 13: The Node-Based UI Library Gets Faster</a></span> — The first major release in two years cuts re-renders in large flows, with new <code>useNode</code> and <code>useEdge</code> hooks. <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192976/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">Vue Flow has joined the xyflow family</a> too.</p>\n<p>xyflow </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192977/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/kg7bhtjdxtgjgocpxf3z.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 100%;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192977/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">SlickGrid 6.0</a> <em>(above)</em> – Long-standing data grid component. Now with zero dependencies, plus <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192978/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">pinning on all four sides</a> and sticky rows and columns. Examples and introductions <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192979/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">on the project homepage</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192980/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">XState v6 Beta</a> – First beta of the state machine/actor library's next major version. The core API is considered stable and you can try it with <code>xstate@beta</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🕹️ <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192981/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Excalibur 1.0</a> – After 13 years, the 2D TypeScript game engine reaches v1 with 2D lighting, a plugin API, and a rebuilt Web Audio mixer. <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192982/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Sample games here</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🕹️ <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192983/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">JSNES 3.0</a> – JavaScript NES emulator. Now has a cycle-accurate core that passes all 134 'AccuracyCoin' tests. There are <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192984/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">playable demos here</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192985/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">type-fest 6.0</a> – Popular collection of essential TypeScript types (<a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192986/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">listed here</a>). v6.0 adds 120 new types and requires TypeScript 6.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192987/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Fontkit 3.0</a> – Advanced font engine for Node and the browser. Now ESM-only, with a new <code>core</code> export so you can ship only the formats needed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192988/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">dts-bundle-generator 10.0</a> – A tool to tree-shake and bundle a project's TypeScript declarations into one file.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px;\">\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📰 Classifieds</p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📄 Parsing PDFs for a RAG or invoice pipeline? <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192989/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Foxit compares 10 document parsing APIs</a> on output format, billing units and accuracy claims.</p>\n<p>🌙 Agents can test mobile code at 3am. <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192990/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Expo Simulators</a> gives them cloud simulators, so the loop runs while you sleep.</p>\n<p>Stop being the middle manager for your coding agents. <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192991/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">CodeAF</a> runs the org, and only what no manager can settle reaches you. #1 on DeepSWE.</p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0;\"><p>📢  Elsewhere in the ecosystem</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192992/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/ldgdhmhflcadzj94nlv6.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 100%;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192992/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">You Don't Need jQuery</a>, the long-standing guide to replacing jQuery methods with plain JavaScript, has had a big <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193005/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">v3.0 update</a>. <em>Internet Explorer</em> fallbacks are out in favor of modern one-liners like <code>el.replaceChildren()</code>, <code>structuredClone()</code>, and <code>Promise.withResolvers()</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Adam Argyle shares <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192993/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">PropFLIP</a>, a take on <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192994/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">FLIP (First, Last, Invert, Play) animations</a> where four <code>@property</code> custom properties do the inverting and a CSS transition does the playing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>AWS has released <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192995/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">AWS Emulator</a>, an <em>official</em> local emulator you can point your AWS SDK or CLI at, covering hundreds of its service interfaces. It's largely aimed at agents, and <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192996/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">was built with them too</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🎂 Happy birthday to <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192997/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Svelte</a>! Svelte turned ten years old on Monday (Rich Harris made <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/192998/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">its first commit</a> on November 16, 2016).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n</td></tr>\n</table>\n",
